Day 12: Installing Anaconda

Anaconda is best for data science for now.
It has most data science packages already installed.

Or use Jupyter Notebook.

In Jupyter you can run Python code or write markdown notes.
You can also use interactive widgets like buttons and forms.

How to set up a dataset:

99% of the time we go to Kaggle.

To import a dataset into Jupyter Notebook, use pandas:

import pandas as pd
pd.read_csv("filename")

How to create a virtual environment:

When we start Jupyter Notebook, there is already an environment with many packages.

Sometimes we want a new environment so we download only the packages we need.

From command line (for Anaconda):

conda create --name test

To activate the conda env:
conda activate test

Jupyter Notebook itself is a library.

conda install -c anaconda jupyter

To deactivate, just write:
conda deactivate

For beginners, use Kaggle or Google Colab.

Using Kaggle:
Create an account, find a dataset, and open a notebook in the browser.

Using Google Colab:
Open colab.research.google.com, create a new notebook, and run Python in the cloud.
